## Domain

<!-- ~200w. DRAFTED. -->

*Magic: The Gathering* is a trading card game with over 30,000 mechanically unique cards! The most popular
way to play magic is the **Commander** format, which imposes three rules on the player while they are building
a deck. It must include exactly **100 different cards** (excluding basic lands); it is led by one **commander**, a card that sits outside the other 99 and restricts them - every card in the deck must draw its colors from a subset of the commander's five-color *color identity*; Within that color identity, **any card ever printed** may be put in the deck (barring ~50 banned cards).

A natural consequence of the abundance of options, is that players struggle to **cut** cards out of their deck.
There are always shiny new cards that they want to add to the deck, but its never fun to take out a good card to make space. Part of making good cuts requires knowing which cards are only good *together*: a **synergy package**, and finding those packages automatically is the problem this project tackles.

## Data

<!-- ~300w. DRAFTED. ** FILE SIZES NEED VERIFYING - carried over from the old docx, rounded there. ** -->

We assembled four sources into one SQLite database (185 MB; ~560 MB with derived matrices).

| source | what it gives | size |
|---|---|---|
| **Scryfall** | every printed card: cost, type, oracle text, color identity, ~100 fields | 31,830 cards, 20 MB |
| **Scryfall Tagger** | crowdsourced mechanic labels (themes, tribes, actions) - our only ground-truth-ish account of what a card *does* | 376,800 tags over ~950 types, 55 MB |
| **Archidekt** | user-submitted decklists | 13,207 decks over 1,001 commanders, 110 MB |
| **EDHREC** | aggregate metagame statistics and commander themes | 0.5 MB |

*Tagger labels are deliberately **held out of every clustering step**, which lets us use them later as
an independent check rather than an input.

A couple of notes on the data: There are millions of decklists which at 2s/d would take a couple of months to scrape, so we only took a sample. a bredth sample of the top 1000 most played commanders according to EDHREC, with ~10 decks per commander, and a depth sample of ~1000 decks on 5 specific commanders. To balance this discrepancy during analyses, we apply an inverse-probability deck weighting. we should also note that most of the people who play the game don't upload their decks to this site, and decks on the site aren't necessarily played in real games (could just be brainstorms) so there's a built in bias.

About the cards themselves, card popularity falls off logarithmically with a thin tail.

![Card popularity](Images/fig1_card_popularity.png)
*Figure 1 - Every card in the analysis pool, ordered by how many decks play it.*

the median card in the analysis pool appears in only 14 decks, so to get sufficient evidence we require a card to appear in at least 3 decks (18,979 of 31,830 survive), and after the filtering described in Chapter 1, 16,026 make it into the final graph. Another deliberate design choice we made is that the **commanders themselves are absent** from the decklist records - the single most defining card of each deck contributes nothing to co-occurrence. Otherwise the graphs would center around the 1000 commanders instead of finding the inner communities and themes between decks. The remaining biases are mechanical rather than sampling, and dismantling them is the subject of Question 1.

---

## 1. Two-card combo - measuring synergy between cards

<!-- ~750w. DRAFTED. Source: docs/devlog/6.1, 6.1b, 6.3d. -->

#### Problem Description

Which pairs of cards actually belong together? Sharing a single deck is weak evidence of actual synergy: the color-identity rule alone guarantees that two white cards meet far more often than a white card and a blue one. Not to mention a pre-constructed deck ships 100 cards together, and staple cards like *Sol Ring* sits in 82.5% of decks and therefore correlate with everything. We needed a number that sees through all of these factors.

#### Our Solution

We turned the 13,207 decklists into a graph of cards. Each card is a node, each surviving pair is an
edge, and an edge's weight is our estimate of how strongly those two cards want to be in a deck
together. Everything in Question 2 is built on this graph, so a lot of our effort went here.

We scored every pair with three association metrics, each fixing a flaw in the one before:

1. **Co-occurrence** - how many decks contain both cards. Simple, and dominated by card and deck popularity.
2. **Lift** - `(joint·N)/(count_A·count_B)`: how many times more often two cards meet than they would
   if decks were assembled at random. It reads directly - "4× more often than chance" - but it treats
   two decks and two hundred decks as equally convincing.
3. **t-score** - `(observed − expected)/√observed`. Instead of asking how far above chance a pair
   sits, it asks how *well-evidenced* that gap is, so a handful of decks can no longer produce a
   headline number.

We also adjusted the meaning of `expected` in t-score. The naive version divides by all ~13,000 decks, which quietly assumes any card could have gone into any deck. We instead divide by the decks *legally able* to run each card, so two white cards are credited only for meeting more often than two white cards normally do.

**We derived the following formula for our synergy score: `t-score × log(1 + lift)`.** The t-score
carries the evidence, the lift carries the effect size, and neither alone was good enough.

![Each metric's answer for one card](Images/cards1_metric_comparison.png)
*Figure 2 - Thornbite Staff untaps a creature every time a creature dies, so what it wants is creatures with abilities worth using twice, or other untappers. Co-occurrence answers Sol Ring and Mountain; lift answers cards played in a handful of decks; t-score answers generic equipment and two lands. Only the synergy score finds the mechanic - every card it returns reads "untap target creature".*

A score only ranks pairs, though; it never says which pairings are relationships at all. That
decision turned out to be impossible pairwise (see Results), so we make it **structurally**: an edge
survives only if the two cards keep the same company, measured as the Jaccard overlap of their
neighbour sets. The two jobs are separate - the score says *how strong*, the gate says *whether it
is real*.

#### Evaluation

**Evaluation Criteria**

This was mainly evaluated using our domain knowledge of how magic works. We hand-labelled a keep/drop set of real pairs. A metric works if it keeps pairs a player would call synergy and drops pairs that merely share a
color or a shelf. Specifically we expect generic staple cards (ones that are generally useful and don't need synergies to work) to score low with any other card. inversly cards that want thing A should score very high with cards that supply thing A, but not with cards that only supply thing B (unless A and B are directly related). Also niche cards that are almost never played shouldn't score too high with anything, since we don't have enough evidence for a strong claim.
![Hand-labelled pair examples](Images/cards2_keep_drop.png)
*Figure 3 - What we labelled and why. The first three pairs need each other to function; Abrade and Lightning Bolt do the same job, so they are substitutes rather than partners; Command Tower and Beast Within share decks only because both are widely played.*
[Friends - show our test set, and ask them to rate how synergistic they are]
[Friends - show pairs our synergy score rates highly/poorly, vs baseline random pair rating generator]

**Setup**

All three metrics are built over the same 13,207 decks by `edhcut.analysis.cooccurrence`, with decks
weighted by inverse commander probability and cards that show in pre-constructed decks are down-weighted by how often players actually cut them. 18,979 of 31,830 cards clear the 3-deck floor, and 16,026 survive into the graph.

**Results & Visualization**

Each metric fails in its own direction, and the failures are what built the final formula.

![Lift's top pairs](Images/fig2_lift_fails.png)
*Figure 4 - The four highest lift scores in the entire corpus.*

**Lift is disqualified outright.** Its best pairs in 13,207 decks all sit at exactly **4,402** - the
score two cards get when both appear in 3 decks and never apart. Over 10,000 pairs clear lift 150 on
fewer than 12 decks each. For comparison, the strongest genuine combo we have, *Thassa's Oracle* with
*Demonic Consultation*, scores 64.

![t-score's top pairs](Images/fig3_tscore_fails.png)
*Figure 5 - The four highest t-scores in the corpus, once basic lands are set aside.*

**t-score fails in the mirror direction.** It kills the coincidences dead - all three score under
**1.8**, below every real pair - but its own top four is *Sol Ring*, *Arcane Signet* and *Command
Tower* in every combination, cards in 4,000 to 10,900 decks that go in everything. A tiny relative
excess on a huge count is still many standard deviations.

![The two scores on the labelled pairs](Images/fig4_product_works.png)
*Figure 6 - Our 22 hand-labelled pairs under t-score and under the product. A strip plot is the right display because the only question is whether the three groups occupy separate regions.*

**The product buys most of the difference back.** Under t-score, **6 of the 9** generic pairs outscore
the weakest real pair; multiplying by `log(1 + lift)` cuts that to **2 of 9**. The cost is honest and
visible: one coincidence, *Sheltering Ancient* + *Tolarian Contempt*, climbs back above the weakest
real pair. Neither score separates the groups on its own, which is what the gate is for.

![The Jaccard gate](Images/fig5_jaccard_gate.png)
*Figure 7 - The same pairs by how much their neighbourhoods overlap, with the 0.03 gate marked.*

**The gate decides what the score cannot.** At 0.03 it keeps all 10 synergy pairs and drops 7 of the
9 non-synergy pairs. What survives is one category: *Abrade* + *Lightning Bolt* (0.218) and *Swords
to Plowshares* + *Loran of the Third Path* (0.065). Two red removal spells genuinely do keep the same
company, so this is not a measurement failure but a different relationship - they are *substitutes*
rather than partners, which is what Question 3 takes up.

![The staple detector](Images/fig6_staple_detector.png)
*Figure 8 - Play rate against a card's strongest lift to anything. The shaded box is the exclusion rule.*

Generic staples pass every filter we built, so we caught them by a different property: no
above-chance affinity to *anything*. *Sol Ring*'s strongest lift in the entire format is **1.13**,
against *Lion Sash*'s **104.6**. Thresholding at `max lift < 3.5 and play rate ≥ 10%` selects exactly
12 cards and spares *Evolving Wilds* at 4.84 - and it only works on the gated graph, since before
gating it finds just 6 of the 12.

Finally, the color correction fires where we want and nowhere else. Across 13 hand-classified pairs,
generic same-color pairs collapse by **72-92%** - *Swords to Plowshares* + *Felidar Retreat* falls
from t = 8.2 to 0.6 - while real synergy loses at most a few percent. The three colorless pairs move
by **0.002-0.12%**, which is the algebraic check passing: a colorless card has no legality
restriction, so the corrected formula must reduce to the uncorrected one, and any real movement there
would have been a bug.

**Impediments**

Most of our parameters are mitigations rather than free choices, so they are collected here:

| problem | what we did |
|---|---|
| Thin evidence - the median card is in 14 decks | require a card in ≥3 decks; below that, a package and a coincidence look identical |
| Graph too dense to cluster | keep each card's 15 strongest edges, union-symmetrised so a niche card keeps its link to a popular one (exact number is arbitrary) |
| Borderline edges | Jaccard gate at ≥ 0.03; we read the 0.02–0.03 band by hand and found it almost entirely staple-to-staple noise, 82% of it linking unrelated communities |
| Staples merging unrelated clusters | exclude the 5 basic lands and the 12 detected format staples as graph nodes |
| Pre-constructed decks - buy one, swap ten cards, and you have donated thousands of meaningless pairs | down-weight individual precon cards by how often players actually cut them |

**Tools**

Algorithms: *lift, t-score, color-conditioned null model, Jaccard neighbourhood overlap.*

Libraries: *pandas, numpy, scipy, SQLite.*

