## 2. "This would go great in my dragon deck!" - recovering card packages from co-occurrence

<!-- ~850w. DRAFTED. Source: docs/devlog/6.3, 6.3c, 6.3d; notebooks symNMF_communities.ipynb, louvain_communities.ipynb. -->

#### Problem Description

A good magic deck picks a theme and maybe a subtheme, and deliberately adds cards from **packages** related to these themes. since you draw only a portion of the entire deck in each game, its important to have redundant synergies, and packages promise you that cards within them work well together. So: can those packages be recovered from co-occurrence alone, by a system that doesn't know magic and has never read a card? And once we identified them, does a card belong to one package or to several at once?

#### Our Solution

We ran community detection on the synergy score graph from Question 1 (16,026 cards, 158,294 edges). We used and compared two algorithms:

1. **Louvain** gives a **hard partition**: every card lands in exactly one community, chosen to
   maximise modularity. It is fast, deterministic enough, and easy to reason about.
2. **symNMF** gives a **soft** one. It factorises the graph as `S ≈ H·Hᵀ` with `H ≥ 0`, so each row
   of `H` is a card's membership spread across `k` topics. A card can belong to three packages with
   weights, rather than being forced into the best one.

To test if the communities make sense in the context of known themes, we labeled each
community with mechanic tags from **Scryfall Tagger**, scoring every tag by `share × log(1 + lift)` and
requiring it to be both common (in ≥10% of the community) and distinctive (lift ≥ 8 over at least 4
cards). Both halves are necessary: `share` alone picks vacuous tags that every card carries, `lift`
alone picks freak tags that happen to sit on four cards. **Those tags never enter the clustering.**
They are a completely independent description of what each card does, so if they line up with
communities built purely from "these cards appeared in the same deck", that agreement means
something.

| parameter | value | why |
|---|---|---|
| topics `k` | 200 | chosen to be comparable to Louvain's community count on the same graph |
| membership floor | share ≥ 10%, at most 5 topics | below a tenth of a card's mass, membership is noise |
| k-core floor | k = 4 | a card must have 4 edges *inside* a community to belong to it; tested against a hand-labelled set, it removed 11 of 12 cards we had flagged as misplaced and 0 of 12 known core members |
| seed | 42, fixed | symNMF is non-deterministic - see Evaluation Criteria |

#### Evaluation

**Evaluation Criteria**

The system is given no game knowledge, so success means the packages it finds are recognisable
*afterwards*. Three checks, in increasing strictness.

First, the held-out tags have to name the communities specifically. A community that gets a sharp
mechanical label is a real package; one whose best tag is weak gets labelled `(generic)`, which
doubles as an automatic detector for the low-quality communities.
[Friends - ask them to match tags to communities, see if they agree with our algorithm]

Second, the result has to beat chance. Cards in the same community tend to share colors, but colors
are also the format's legality rule, so we compare every community's color purity against
same-size random draws from the same pool.

Third, it has to be stable. symNMF starts from a random initialisation, so a result that only exists
at seed 42 is not a result. We refit at five seeds and compared, using a **degree-preserving
rewiring** of the same graph as the floor - same cards, same number of connections each, structure
destroyed - because a stability number means nothing without knowing what a structureless graph
scores.

**Setup**

The graph enters clustering with 16,026 nodes and 158,294 edges, median degree 14. symNMF converges
in 194 iterations, 24 seconds. Louvain produces 244 communities against symNMF's 200. We also carry
a standing guard: for each of our over-sampled commanders we take its *signature* cards - the ones
in at least half of that commander's decks - and check what share of them land in a single
community, so an oversampled commander cannot quietly become a "package".

**Results & Visualization**

The communities are real, and they are legible. Level-0 output includes Equipment, Landfall, Extra
Turns, Shrines, Cascade, Deserts, Allies and twenty-odd typal packages, all named by tags the
clustering never saw.

![The community graph](Images/fig10_community_graph.png)
*Figure 9 - The 14 largest named packages. Cards are pulled together by edge weight, so distance on the page is synergy. A force-directed layout is the right chart here because the claim is about grouping, and grouping is the thing it makes visible.*

Our own title case is the best illustration. Dragons do not come out as one package but as **three**,
and the split is not by creature type - it is by how the deck plays. Community 63 (79 cards) is the
aggressive Rakdos core, Dragon Tempest and Scourge of Valkas. Community 87 (62 cards) is the big
multicolor ramp shell, Lathliss and Atarka and Klauth. Community 137 (41 cards) is Grixis control,
Crux of Fate and Silumgar and Junji. Asking "would this go great in my dragon deck?" turns out to
have three different answers.

![The three dragon packages](Images/cards3_dragons.png)
*Figure 10 - The most-played members of each dragon package.*

The soft/hard difference is the sharpest finding. Cards average **1.45** memberships and **41%
belong to more than one community**, which is structure a hard partition cannot represent at all.
*Mondrak, Glory Dominus* is the clearest case. It doubles your tokens, it is an expensive staple that's played in high-power decks, and it is a Phyrexian - and the model independently places it 47% in the
token payoffs, 22% among the format's powerful staples and 21% in Phyrexian typal. One package per
thing the card does, recovered without reading a word of it.

![Mondrak's three packages](Images/cards4_multi_membership.png)
*Figure 11 - Mondrak and the three packages it belongs to, with the share of its membership in each.*

![Mondrak's connections across three packages](Images/fig11_multi_membership_graph.png)
*Figure 12 - Mondrak's own connections, drawn over the graph. Everything it does not connect to is greyed out. The three packages barely touch each other, which is why no single label can describe it.*

A hard partition has to pick one of those three and discard the other two.

![Packages per card by EDHREC rank](Images/fig9_packages_by_popularity.png)
*Figure 13 - The 10,000 most-played cards, in bins of fifty by EDHREC rank, against the number of packages they belong to. Past rank 10,000 the line stays flat at about 1.4.*

The effect is strongest exactly where it matters: the top 2,000 cards average **2.10** packages
against **1.52** at rank 10,000, because a card reaches that many decks precisely when several
different strategies have a use for it. Those popular, multi-purpose cards are the ones a player
actually agonises over cutting, and they are the ones a hard partition describes worst.

Color purity is **56.6%** across the 200 communities, against a same-size random null of **19.4%** -
so the structure is far from a color artifact, though colors clearly contribute. The commander guard
holds: Krenko's signature cards are 72.4% concentrated, Kyler's 41.7%, Yenna's 39.3%.

![Color purity against the null](Images/fig7_color_purity.png)
*Figure 14 - How color-consistent each community is, against same-size random draws from the same pool of cards.*

The seeds agree too. We refit the whole thing at five different random seeds, and on average
**76%** of the community structure is reproduced exactly. On the rewired graph, where we destroyed
the structure but kept every card and every connection count, the same measurement gives **43%** -
so most of what we are seeing is in the data rather than in the starting point.

**Impediments**

We tried several methods before landing on **symNMF** with the current symmetry metric.
**Girvan-Newman** was our first attempt but cost roughly 65 minutes per subgraph, which made it unusable at this scale. **k-clique percolation** ignores edge weights entirely, so it cannot distinguish a t-score of 30 from one of 2; on our graph it percolated into a single **15,479-node blob** and returned barely more than one
community per card. An earlier **NMF** topic model produced 40 topics that never read as archetypes.

Two more subtle ones. **Degree normalisation** improved every metric we could measure but produced
visibly worse communities - hyper-specific small ones and incoherent large ones. A **3-color bias survives**: controlling for popularity, 31% of three-color cards end up in no community at all, against 3-6% for every other color count. A three-color card is only legal in decks whose commander covers all three, and that pool spans many unrelated archetypes, so its partners spread too thin to clear the k-core floor.

**Tools**

Algorithms: *symNMF, Louvain, k-core, modularity, adjusted Rand index.*

Libraries: *networkx, scipy, numpy, ipysigma.*

