## 3. Reading the card explains the card - text similarity vs. co-occurrence

<!-- ~850w. DRAFTED. Source: docs/devlog/6.2, 6.4; notebooks embeddings_exploration.ipynb. -->

#### Problem Description

Question 2's model never read a single card. This one reads nothing else. Both produce a list of
"cards like this one", so the obvious question is whether they produce the *same* list - and if not,
what the difference is. The answer turns out to be a distinction the project needed anyway: a
recommender that suggests a **replacement** for a card is doing something completely different from
one that suggests what to build **around** it. Cutting a card raises the first question; keeping it
raises the second.

#### Our Solution

We built a second similarity measure that never looks at a decklist. Every card becomes a vector made
only of what is printed on it, and cards are compared by the angle between those vectors. One
algorithm does the work:

1. **TF-IDF + truncated SVD** - `TF-IDF` scores a word by how often it appears on *this* card against
   how many cards use it at all, so a word every card carries counts for almost nothing and a rare one
   counts for a lot. That gives each card a vector with one entry per word in the game's vocabulary,
   far too many to compare directly, so `SVD` compresses it down to 64 numbers that keep the
   directions the cards actually differ along.

A Magic card looks busy, but it holds only five things worth reading, and every one of them sits in
the same place on every card ever printed: a **name**, a **mana cost** to play it, a **type line**, a
box of **abilities** saying what it does, and for creatures a **power and toughness**. The art, the
italic flavour text and the set symbol are decoration, and our model ignores all of it.

![The parts of a Magic card](Images/card_anatomy.png)
*Figure 15 - The five parts of a card our model reads, on a card that is also a member of Question 2's dragon package. The flavour text and set symbol are ignored.*

Those four kinds of information matter unequally, so each goes into its own block, is scaled to the
same length, and is then weighted:

| block | weight | why |
|---|---|---|
| rules text | 0.6 | what the card actually does, and the only block that describes behaviour |
| type line | 0.15 | creature vs artifact vs enchantment separates cards that read alike but play differently |
| mana cost | 0.15 | a one-mana and a six-mana version of the same effect are not replacements for each other |
| power / toughness | 0.1 | creatures only; redistributed onto the other blocks otherwise |

Two details do most of the work. We wrote a tokenizer that keeps mana symbols such as `{T}` and `{G}`
as words, since the standard one discards anything shorter than two characters and would throw away a
mana ability entirely. And the type line gets its **own** block rather than being pooled with the
rules text: on a card whose text is one short sentence, a single shared word like "Human" otherwise
outweighs the ability itself.

Separately, we classified every card into one of **18 functional roles** - removal, ramp, draw,
boardwipe and so on - two independent ways: from crowdsourced mechanic tags, and from regular
expressions over the card's own rules text. That gives us a way to *test* whether "cards that do the
same job" behave differently from "cards that need each other", rather than just asserting it.

#### Evaluation

**Evaluation Criteria**

Success is that the text measure returns cards a player would accept as **replacements**, and that it
demonstrably answers a different question from co-occurrence.

The first half is judged by reading the output on real cards, which is the only honest test for a
similarity measure with no ground truth. We also keep a standing check: *Cultivate*'s nearest
neighbour must be *Kodama's Reach*. They are the same card with a different name, so any method that
misses that is broken - and both of our measures find it independently.

The second half is measurable. Using groups taken from Scryfall Tagger, which never entered either
model, we compare **functional categories** (all the boardwipes) against **synergy packages** (the
landfall cards) on both axes at once. If the two signals really answer different questions, the two
kinds of group should separate on one axis and not on the other.

[Friends - ask them to name a replacement for a card and a partner for it, and see which of our two lists matches which question]

**Setup**

Text vectors cover all 31,623 commander-legal cards - every card, whether or not anyone has ever put
it in a deck, which is exactly what co-occurrence cannot do. The role classifier runs over all 31,830
cards and its two layers can both read 13,166 of them. For the category-versus-package comparison we
used eleven Tagger groups and every pair among each group's fourteen most-played members; sampling at
random from a 4,600-card tag mostly returns two obscure cards that have never met, which measures
nothing.

**Results & Visualization**

The text measure finds replacements, and the clearest way to see it is beside the other list.

![Skullclamp's two neighbour lists](Images/cards5_substitutes_vs_complements.png)
*Figure 16 - Skullclamp's four nearest cards under each signal. Text returns four other cheap Equipment - things you could play instead. Co-occurrence returns the sacrifice engine it is played with, and not one of them is an Equipment.*

Both lists are correct answers to different questions. Every card in the text list is a cheap
Equipment that buffs a creature; every card in the co-occurrence list is part of the sacrifice engine
*Skullclamp* draws cards off. A player asking "what do I put in this slot instead?" wants the first; a
player asking "is this card doing anything here?" wants the second.

The two signals are close to unrelated, and the geometry shows why.

![The text space](Images/fig13_text_space.png)
*Figure 17 - The text space projected down to two dimensions. The inset zooms in on Skullclamp, where its text neighbours are packed so tightly they are invisible at full scale.*

Laid out purely by what cards say, *Skullclamp*'s eight nearest cards by text sit at cosine similarity
**0.97 and above** - so close together that they occupy one point on the map and need an inset to be
seen at all. The eight cards it is actually *played* with are scattered to opposite corners of the
same picture. Neither space is wrong; they are not measuring the same thing.

**Functional categories are not synergy packages, and the gap is not subtle.** Across eleven groups,
the five functional categories sit at a median lift of **0.99** - which is to say *exactly chance*, as
though the game had dealt them out at random - while the six synergy packages sit at **9.15**. Not one
category clears 2.5x and not one package falls below 3.5x.

![Lift within each group](Images/fig12_group_lift.png)
*Figure 18 - Every pair among each group's fourteen most-played cards, by how much more than chance the two are played together.*

Text similarity cannot make that distinction at all. The groups that read most alike are goblins (a
package, 0.78) and counterspells (a category, 0.71); the two that read least alike are boardwipes (a
category, 0.11) and lifegain (a package, 0.29). Knowing that two cards read the same tells you nothing
about whether they belong in a deck together.

This also settles two things the earlier questions left open. *Starfall Invocation* ended up in no
community at all because every other boardwipe had been eliminated from its neighbourhood during
sparsification - not a filter failure, but the filter working, since a boardwipe's real relationships
are to the cards it protects. And *Abrade* and *Lightning Bolt* survived every filter in Question 1
because two red removal spells genuinely do keep the same company without ever needing each other.

One last thing the text space told us, which is really a fact about the game rather than about our
model. Magic writes in its own dialect, and it is a narrow one.

![What Magic's rules text is made of](Images/fig19_oracle_wordcloud.png)
*Figure 19 - Every word in the rules text of all 31,491 commander-legal cards, sized by how often it appears across the whole game.*

The word *creature* appears on **69.4%** of all cards, *target* on 39.4%, *control* on 30.7% and
*turn* on 29.5%. These are the words a card is obliged to use to say anything at all, and a standard
English stopword list - which strips "the" and "of" - leaves every one of them untouched. So a real
share of what our text vectors measure is vocabulary the entire game shares rather than anything
specific to the card in front of them, which is the deeper reason terse cards came out so badly.

**Impediments**

**A land that taps for white and a creature that taps for white have almost the same rules text.**
Early on, 14 of *Avacyn's Pilgrim*'s 15 nearest cards were lands. No amount of statistics can discover
that a land is never a replacement for a spell - a Commander deck runs a fixed ~37 lands regardless of
what else is in it - so we encoded that boundary directly, and share the same filter with Question 1.

**Terse cards carry almost no signal.** A vanilla creature's rules text is one or two words, so
whichever words survive dominate its vector completely. This is why the type line had to be kept
separate from the rules text, and it remains a real limit rather than a solved problem: for the
plainest cards, text similarity is mostly reporting mana cost and creature type.

**Most pairs of cards have never met.** Our first pass at the category-versus-package comparison
sampled pairs at random and returned a median lift of zero for almost every group - not because the
groups are unrelated, but because two cards drawn at random from a 4,600-card tag are usually two
obscure cards that share no deck at all. Restricting to each group's most-played members is what made
the comparison mean anything.

**Text similarity has no idea what a card is worth.** Two cards can read almost identically and be
separated by a decade of power creep, and the vector cannot tell. That is the single biggest reason we
would not ship replacement suggestions from text alone.

**Tools**

Algorithms: *TF-IDF, truncated SVD, cosine similarity, t-SNE, regex classification.*

Libraries: *scikit-learn, numpy, pandas.*

---

## Future Work

**Question 1 - measuring synergy.** Each confounder we removed was handled on its own; we never
measured how much of the raw co-occurrence signal each one accounts for. A decomposition would say
whether color legality or product shells or plain popularity does the most damage, and would tell a
future version where to spend its effort. The structural gate also deserves competition: neighbourhood
overlap was the first structural statistic we tried and it worked, which is not the same as it being
the best one.

**Question 2 - finding packages.** The number of packages, 200, was chosen to be comparable to what
the hard partition produced, and the evidence suggests it is too coarse: a granularity sweep peaked
at roughly 1,200 groups. Running that properly would cost about half an hour per fit, and would tell
us where a package stops being an archetype and starts being noise. Two known biases also remain
fixable rather than fixed - the gate and the membership floor know nothing about color legality, which
is why 31% of three-color cards end up in no package at all, and raising the three-deck floor to ten
would remove most of the remaining chaff at the cost of genuinely narrow cards.

**Question 3 - reading cards.** The vocabulary in Figure 19 is the clearest lead. Our text model
treats rules text as English when it plainly is not, so the obvious next version is built for Magic
specifically: its own stopword list, and a decomposition fitted to this dialect rather than to
English, so that all 64 dimensions are spent on what separates cards instead of on what every card is
obliged to say. The other experiment we would run is to predict a card's package from its rules text
alone. That turns Question 2's output into labels and Question 3's vectors into features, and
because the two signals are close to independent, the accuracy would be a real measurement of how much
of a deck's structure is legible from the cards themselves.

**The tool this was all for.** The knowledge base exists to power a cut recommender: score each of a
deck's 99 cards by how weakly it attaches to the packages that deck is built around, and suggest the
loosest ones. What makes that testable is that ground truth already exists in our data. Thousands of
our decks began as a preconstructed product, so we can see **which cards real players actually cut**
when they upgraded one - and check whether our score would have cut the same ones.

## Conclusion

Three questions, one answer that kept getting sharper. Co-occurrence between cards is mostly artifact:
the format's own legality rules, the products people buy, and the handful of cards that go in
everything explain most of it, and we proved that no statistic computed from a pair of cards in
isolation can separate the real relationships from the rest. What finally worked was to stop asking
about the pair and start asking about the company each card keeps.

What survives that filtering is genuinely structural. A system given no card text, no types, and no
rules of the game recovered goblins, landfall, equipment and three separate kinds of dragon deck, and
we could name them using labels it had never seen.

We should be clear about what we did not do: the knowledge base is built and validated, the tool that
would consume it is not. What we have is a thorough analysis of a dataset rather than a shipped
product, and the analysis is where all the interesting content turned out to live anyway.

## Afterword

It was increadibly fun to work on this project. As someone who is both visually oriented and constantly thinking about this hobby, it's super cool seeing these connections i've been developing in my head take form on these graphs. It's incredible how simple and accessible exploring the data is these days, and i'm definitly intending to finish this tool and uploading it to the internet for other people to use. There is a widespread epidemic of EDHREC-ification, where deckbuilding data isn't accessible enough, so people go to edhrec and put in only genericly popular cards, instead of looking for cool niche synergy pieces.

